{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem asks specifically what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. SRC_001's evidence-of-gain criterion — 'detection of basic errors that experts habitually overlook' — is exactly the ambiguity the problem demands be resolved: catching trivial errors is consistent with both a genuine reliability improvement AND rote checklist compliance that merely happens to land on the right answer without deeper understanding. The target offers no within-evaluation probe (e.g., performance on novel items where the checklist does not map, or error-pattern analysis separating mechanistic tick-box agreement from substantive judgment) to separate these. It thus answers the evidence-distinguishing half of the problem by restating the very phenomenon to be distinguished.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A panel using a checklist catches the same 'basic' errors on items where the checklist's steps happen to align with the verifiable outcome, but fails equally basic errors on isomorphic items whose surface features do not trigger the listed steps — demonstrating the panel is merely complying rather than genuinely more reliable, yet would satisfy SRC_001's evidential criterion.","src_001_criterion":"detection of basic errors experts overlook","src_001_fails_to_separate":"checklist-compliance coincidence from genuine reliability gain"}],"target":"7b3cb62e31f6645f1a3daeea1d47a7af2bcd585ab27b92b06755425534895cd8"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's dispositive evidence — the 'expert-checklist performance dip' (the expert doing worse when given the checklist) — does not bear on the claim being argued. The claim compares a panel of trained non-experts WITH a checklist against a single expert WITHOUT one. Whether giving the checklist to the expert helps or hurts establishes only the checklist's effect on expert cognition; it is silent on whether the panel's aggregated, trained, checklist-following judgments beat the expert's intuition. A ceiling that constrains the expert is not shown to constrain the panel's net reliability unless the panel is operating at that same ceiling, which the target never establishes. The core inference ('if the expert's intuition exceeds that ceiling, the panel is objectively less reliable') is a non sequitur absent evidence tying panel performance to the checklist's ceiling rather than to the panel's collective judgment.","counterexample":[{"expert_unaided":0.7,"expert_with_checklist":0.55,"panel_with_checklist":0.8,"scenario":"Expert-with-checklist reliability drops to 0.55 (from 0.70 unaided), yet the trained non-expert panel using that same checklist averages 0.80 because the panel's aggregation and training offset the checklist's constraining effect on any single member. The 'dip' is real, but the claim ('panel more reliable than single expert') is still true, refuting the target's purported falsifying evidence."}],"target":"8e23d939b4cc6900e42f295c368aa0689a56ec6f101e284145fe250472eecae2"}]}